\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Hypercube fields and the missing operator correspondence}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{Kluberg-Stern, Morel, Napoly and Petersson block
the fine lattice into hypercubes and use
explicit Clifford coefficient matrices to
transform the complex fields. Their site
phase is fixed by (15), and (20)–(21) give
the forward and inverse transformations with
their normalization. The resulting free
spin/flavor action includes a second-difference
term \citet{pp. 450–453, (15), (19)–(22)}{klubergsternmorelnapolypetersson1983flavours}.
The axial generator in (25) acts on both spin and flavor.
Its continuous transformation preserves the massless kinetic
action. A nonzero mass term breaks this axial symmetry and
preserves the vector symmetry
\citet{p. 453, (25) and the following paragraph}{klubergsternmorelnapolypetersson1983flavours}.}

\p{To compare that action, a fine staggered shift,
or the unreduced Sharatchandra–Thun–Weisz
operator with the present cochains requires
a specified fine-site map, compatible boundary
conditions, gauge transport paths and a proved
operator intertwiner. A coefficient-symbol
identification alone supplies none of the
site or transport data. The correspondence
remains open at that scope, as in
\ref{fdip-009X}. The completed right-action
algebras, ranks and internal column
intertwiner here do not yield a taste,
generation, flavor, chiral or doubler-free
interpretation.}
